---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AnswerFacts from '../components/AnswerFacts.astro';
import SourceLine from '../components/SourceLine.astro';
import { FIX_FORM } from '../lib/group-requests.mjs';
export const page = { door: "care", topic: "health", label: "Flu and COVID shots", summary: "Who should get one, when, and what it costs" };
const AAFP = 'https://www.aafp.org/assets/image/upload/v1788277985/pdf_2026_through_2027_influenza_rsv_sars_covid_2_guidance.pdf';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const ACOG = 'https://www.acog.org/news/2026/09/acog-releases-2026-27-respiratory-virus-season-immunization-recommendations';
// Who-should-get-one and cost sources, each with the day we checked it (#453).
const adviceSources = [
  { url: 'https://www.cdc.gov/flu/vaccines/vaccinations.html', checked: '2026-10-29', name: 'CDC' },
  { url: AAFP, checked: '2026-10-29', name: 'American Academy of Family Physicians' },
  { url: ACOG, checked: '2026-10-29', name: 'ACOG' },
  { url: 'https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/flu-vaccines', checked: '2026-10-29', name: 'Medicare' },
];
---
<Base help title="Flu and COVID shots in Monongalia County: who, when and cost" description="Who should get a flu or COVID shot, when, and what it costs. Book at the Monongalia County Health Department in Morgantown. Medicare pays in full, and children who qualify get shots free.">
  <h1>Where can I get a flu or COVID shot in Mon County?</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Monongalia County Health Department gives flu and COVID shots to anyone 6 months and older. Call <a href="tel:3045985119">304-598-5119</a> to book. Shots are free for many children, including children with no insurance or with WV Medicaid or WVCHIP, and the clinic has lower prices based on income.</p>

  <AnswerFacts slug="monchd-flu-and-covid-shots" linkLabel="Health department shots page" />

  <h2>Who should get a flu shot?</h2>
  <p>Almost everyone 6 months and older, every fall. Federal health officials (<a href="https://www.cdc.gov/flu/vaccines/vaccinations.html">CDC</a>), family doctors (<a href={AAFP}>AAFP, PDF</a>) and children's doctors (<a href="https://www.healthychildren.org/English/news/Pages/aap-(influenza)-flu-prevention-recommendations-for-2026-27.aspx">AAP</a>) all agree on this. The CDC says September and October are good times to get one.</p>
  <p>A flu shot matters most if you are 65 or older, pregnant or just had a baby, have a child under 5, or have a health problem like asthma, diabetes or heart disease. These people are more likely to get very sick from the flu (<a href="https://www.cdc.gov/flu/highrisk/chronic-conditions/">CDC</a>).</p>
  <p>If you are 65 or older, the CDC and family doctors say a stronger flu shot made for older adults is a better choice. Ask for one by name: high-dose, adjuvanted or recombinant. Family doctors say that if the place you go does not have one, get the regular shot rather than wait.</p>

  <h2>What about a COVID shot?</h2>
  <p>Here the groups do not all say the same thing, so we tell you who says what.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Family doctors (<a href={AAFP}>AAFP, PDF</a>)</strong> recommend a COVID shot this season for all adults, and two doses, six months apart, for people 65 and older.</li>
    <li><strong>Doctors who care for pregnant women (<a href={ACOG}>ACOG</a>)</strong> recommend one if you are pregnant, just had a baby, or are thinking about getting pregnant.</li>
    <li><strong>Federal health officials (CDC)</strong> have not posted new COVID shot advice for this season. Their <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/covid/vaccines/index.html">COVID shot page</a> was last updated in July 2025.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a child, or if you have a health problem or a weak immune system, ask your doctor or a pharmacist what is right for you.</p>

  <h2>Will it cost me?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Medicare:</strong> pays for a <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/flu-vaccines">flu shot</a> and a <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/covid-19-vaccine">COVID shot</a>. You pay nothing if the provider accepts Medicare.</li>
    <li><strong>Children 18 and younger:</strong> free through Vaccines for Children if they have no insurance, insurance that does not cover shots, WV Medicaid or WVCHIP, or are American Indian or Alaska Native.</li>
    <li><strong>No insurance:</strong> the health department's pages do not list a price for adults. They say prices are lower for people with less income. When you call to book, ask what your shot will cost.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The health department's <a href="https://www.monchd.org/services/immunizations">shots page</a> also links to its events calendar, where it lists shot clinics it holds around the community.</p>

  <p>A flu shot is one of a few simple things that help most people stay well. See the others: <a href={`${base}/staying-well/`}>Staying well: a simple list</a>.</p>

  <SourceLine sources={adviceSources} about="who should get a shot and what it costs" />
  <p class="meta">We describe what health groups say. We do not give medical advice. Ask a doctor, nurse or pharmacist about what is right for you. Something out of date? <a href={FIX_FORM}>Tell us</a>.</p>
</Base>
